**Dekker's Algorithm** fixes Attempt 5 by adding a **`tour` tie-breaker** so only *one* thread backs off during a tie:

```cpp
// Conceptual form (Slide 26)
void T0() {
    while (true) {
        setEnSectionCritique();
        while (autreEnSectionCritique()) {
            if (pasMonTour()) {
                clearEnSectionCritique();
                while (pasMonTour())
                    ;
                setEnSectionCritique();
            }
        }
        /* section critique */
        passeMonTour();
        clearEnSectionCritique();
        /* section non-critique */
    }
}
```

**How the backoff loop works:** If both `T0` and `T1` raise `etat[i] = true` simultaneously, `tour` decides who yields. Suppose `tour == 1`: `T0` enters `if (tour == 1)`, **lowers** `etat[0] = false`, and waits politely in `while (tour == 1) ;`. Meanwhile, `T1` sees `tour != 0`, so `T1` **keeps** `etat[1] = true`, exits `while (etat[0])` as soon as `T0` lowers its flag, enters the critical section, and sets `tour = 0` on exit—unblocking `T0`!

